{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node1 runs an internal DNS resolver that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1’s resolver was active and listening on 10.100.255.10:53 over TCP and UDP, and direct DNS queries returned node2’s address, 10.100.255.11. The service, listeners, firewall rules, and answer recovered after node1 restarted, and the final node1 check remained healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5579d9906a77439b","cmd-66364f5cf7344a65","cmd-0b6d15be9dca471c","cmd-ad206580a402424c","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Node2 uses node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2’s persistent NetworkManager profile and /etc/resolv.conf specify node1 at 10.100.255.10 with the internal search domain. Both libc and Python resolution returned 10.100.255.11 before and after node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-76ee3f49fde04d08","cmd-9386352f7ae04aa0","cmd-7ebfbab1ba234362","cmd-78befb1560844d3e"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating and enabling the resolver, applying source-restricted DNS firewall rules, and changing node2’s NetworkManager DNS profile directly served the task. Both restarts were required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. The two package-install attempts failed because no repositories were enabled, with no evidence that they changed package state. No failed services or executor-created temporary residue were observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fcd658899f24796","cmd-24c566804cc24acf","cmd-a41dde052d254ff0","cmd-a4ad32d02267417f","cmd-76ee3f49fde04d08","cmd-e6815acdc54145f8","cmd-7fbedc58d746482a","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. The restart protocol is complete for both managed nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered configuration and lookup behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently agree with the executor evidence.","limitations":["The resolver script and unit contents were redacted, so their implementation details could not be inspected; behavior, binding, firewall restriction, persistence, and recovery were nevertheless directly demonstrated.","Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency was not independently assessed."]}
